### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence indicates that Senator Ron Johnson's claim, made on October 21, 2020, referred specifically to the state of Wisconsin. However, public health data and local reports from that date directly contradict his assertion that the curve of COVID-19 deaths had been flattened. On the day of his speech, Wisconsin reported a high toll of 4,205 new cases and 48 deaths, reflecting a broader national trend of dramatically increasing infections and high daily death rates throughout most of 2020. Because the evidence clearly demonstrates that COVID-19 deaths were not flat, no further information is required to complete this fact-check.

### Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the Claim is `refuted`. Senator Ron Johnson's statement that COVID-19 deaths were "pretty flat" because the curve had been flattened is false. At the time of his statement in October 2020, Wisconsin was experiencing a severe surge in both infections and deaths, and nationwide data similarly showed that COVID-19 cases and deaths were rising dramatically rather than flattening.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Senator Ron Johnson's assertion that COVID-19 deaths were flat due to a flattened curve is refuted by public health data from October 2020. Although Johnson was referring specifically to Wisconsin, reports from the [Milwaukee Journal Sentinel](https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/local/wisconsin/2020/10/21/wisconsin-coronavirus-state-reports-4-205-new-cases-48-deaths/3717200001/) show that on the very day of his statement, the state reported a high toll of 4,205 new cases and 48 deaths. Furthermore, while the [Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)](https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-20-635sp.pdf) defines "flattening the curve" as reducing or delaying the spread of a disease, data from the [National Safety Council](https://injuryfacts.nsc.org/home-and-community/safety-topics/covid-19-cases-in-the-united-states/) indicates that COVID-19 cases were actually increasing dramatically across the United States during most of 2020, with deaths averaging over 1,200 per day.